## 1. Introduction
The hydrogenation of nitroaromatic compounds to the corresponding amines is one of the most utilized catalytic processes in synthetic organic chemistry and bulk chemical industry [1–3]. The hydrogenation also provides a way to reduce the environmental risk of nitroaromatic compounds [4,5]. However, most of metalbased catalysts for the reduction of nitroaromatic compounds highly depend upon the noble metals [6], owing to their scarcity and high cost, and thus hampering their practical applications. Thereby, many scholars devoted themself to develop inexpensive transitional metal materials to replace noble metal catalyst [7,8]. NiFeO is considered to be one of the promising candidates because of its earth abundance, environmental friendliness, long-term stability, magnetic separation in aqueous reaction systems [9]. So far, great efforts have been made in the preparation of NiFeO nanostructure, such as nanodots [10], nanoparticles [11], nanocubes [12], chainlike nanoaggregates [13], which aims to improve its physical and chemical property. Meanwhile, many methods are also employed to optimize NiFeO to facilitate electron transfer, expose active sites, and minimize diffusion barriers, such as: coupled carbon materials [14,15], constructed metal–semiconductor engineering [16], and designed mesoporous architectures [17]. Even so, the application of NiFeO is still restricted by its mediocre catalytic activity in hydrogenation reduction of nitroarenes.
It is well known that NiFeO is a cubic inverse spinel with space group $\mathrm{Fd}3\mathrm{\bar{m}}$ , and their tetrahedral A sites are occupied by $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ whereas the twice as abundant octahedral B sites are randomly occupied by $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ [18]. Due to the unique structure and tunable electron configuration of NiFeO, metal doping has been developed to adjust its catalytic activity and optimize its physicochemical property. According to the presence or absence of doped elements, it is identified as: In situ metal-ion self-doping and heterogeneous ion doping [19]. In general, M doped NiFeO catalyst exhibits promoted activity than that of pristine NiFeO due to modulated electronic structure [20], improved conductivity [21], enhanced redox property [22], optimized the composition [23], and engineered defects [24]. Particularly, oxygen vacancy $(\mathsf{V}_{0})$ not only could act as an electronic charge carrier to significantly improve the electrical conductivity, but also it serves as an extra active site for redox reaction. For example, the defective NiFeO electrocatalyst showed a significant improvement in the overpotential of water oxidation in $1.0\mathrm{{M}}$ KOH, which is closely ascribed to their tuned surface electronic property and lower adsorption energy for $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ [25]; Due to the enlargement of ECSA, the improve of conductivity, the increase of active sites and the acceleration of reaction kinetics, the NiFeO particles with oxygen defects exhibited superior activity in the electrooxidation of urea [26]. In addition, Ni (G)-ferrite $(\mathrm{Ni_{x}F e_{3-x}O_{4-\delta}}$ ; $\mathbf{\deltaX}=0.39,\delta>0\mathrm{\cdot}$ exhibited satisfactory yield and the selectivity in the methanation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . The oxygen-deficient sites of catalysts captured two oxygen atoms of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , while corresponding carbon was reduced, and deposited on their surface, and thus was selectively hydrogenated to form $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ [27]. Therefore, it is easy to understand that the contribution of $\mathsf{V}_{\circ}$ is of greatest importance for the catalytic performance of NiFeO.
Many metal oxides with oxygen vacancies present enhanced catalytic activity for the hydrogenation of nitroaromatic compounds, including $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ [28], $\mathsf{W O}_{2.72}$ [29], $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [30], $\mathrm{Ir/IrO_{x}}$ [31]. Compared to metal-ion self-doped catalysts, heterogeneous element doped catalysts show unexpected superior activity. Su and colleagues reported that nitrogen doped $\mathrm{Ta}_{1.1}0_{1.05}$ and ${\mathsf{C a}}_{2}{\mathsf{N b}}_{2}.$ $0_{7}$ nanocrystals exhibited high conversion rate for the reduction of 4-NP to 4-AP [32,33]. The enhanced activity can be attributed to two factors, one is that the exposure of oxygen defects enhances the adsorption of reactants, and the other is that nitrate species accelerate the formation of metastable iminoborohydride species by binding hydride ions. Later, Su’s group revealed that the doped nitrogen species and oxygen vacancies played the key role in the reduction of nitroaromatic compounds by $N{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}@C$ , respectively. And the catalytic conversion of 4-NP to 4-AP can be achieved only in the presence of the former and the latter in ${\mathsf{N}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}@C$ [34]. The above advances have highlighted the advantages of component control and defect engineering for the design and synthesis of highly active metal oxide-based hydrogenation catalysts. In particular, heterogeneous element introduced may offer the opportunity for the regulation of chemical composition and the creation of oxygen vacancies in metal oxides.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of unique porous materials with molecular-scale cages, which can be used as selfsacrificial templates to encapsulate and confine the metal precursor to fabricate novel binary or ternary metal materials [35,36]. In this paper, mesoporous MNiFeO $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ , Co, Zn, and Mn) NRs are prepared by the thermal conversion of the cation-exchanged $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs precursor, and they served as catalyst for the reduction of 4-NP. The introduce of M improves the redox capability and electron transfer of NiFeO, resulting in promoted catalytic efficiency. CuNiFeO NRs exhibited the best catalytic performance with a specific rate constant $k$ $(7.32\times10^{5}\:\mathrm{\min}^{-1}\:\mathrm{\g}^{-1})$ and TOF $(2.8\bar{9}\times10^{5}\mathrm{h}^{-1},$ . Moreover, mesoporous CuNiFeO NRs also showed an excellent performance in the reduction of other nitroarenes.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals
Terephthalic acid ( $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}B D C}$ ; ${\mathsf{C}}_{8}{\mathsf{H}}_{6}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}$ ) was purchased from TCI Co., Iron (III) chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0);$ , N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF; $(\mathsf{C H}_{3})_{2}\mathsf{N C H O}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ and Sodium hydroxide (NaOH) were obtained from Alfa Aesar Co., Nickel nitrate hexahydrate $\mathrm{'Ni}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\mathrm{)}$ , Cupric acetate monohydrate $(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C O O})_{2}.$ - $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ , Cobalt acetate tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H_{2}O})$ , Zinc acetate dihydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H_{2}O})$ , Manganese acetate tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H_{2}O})$ , 4-Nitrophenol (4-NP; ${\cal C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{NO}_{3};$ ), Hexamethylenetetramine (HMT, $(C_{6}\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{N}_{4})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ , Sodium borohydride $(N a B H_{4})$ and Anhydrous ethanol $(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{5}0\mathsf{H})$ were bought from Sinopharm Chemical Regent Co., 4-Nitrosophenol (4-NSP; ${\cal C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ , 2-Nitrophenol (2-NP; ${\cal C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{NO}_{3}.$ ), 2,4- Dinitrophenol (2,4-DNP; ${\cal C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{2}{\cal O}_{5};$ ), 2,6-Dinitrophenol (2,6-DNP; ${\mathsf{C}}_{6}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}{\mathsf{N}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{5};$ , 2,4,6-Trinitrophenol (TNP; ${\cal C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}0_{7};$ , and 4- Nitroaniline (4-NP; ${\cal C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{N}_{2}{\cal O}_{2};$ ) were acquired from Tianjin Zhiyuan Chemical Reagents Co., Ltd, respectively. All the chemical was analytical grade and used without further purification.
## 2.2. Catalyst preparation
2.2.1. Synthesis of $F e_{2}N i$ -MOFs:
$\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MIL-88 NRs were synthesized according to the reported procedure [37]. Typically, $\ensuremath{0.288\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $0.5433\mathrm{~g~}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $0.4983\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ were mixed in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF by stirring at $25\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , and then $6~\mathrm{mL}$ of NaOH solution $(0.2~\mathrm{M})$ was poured quickly. The obtained mixture was moved to Teflon autoclave liner and heated at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{15\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The resulting products were centrifugated, washed, and dried at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in vacuum for subsequent experiment.
2.2.2. Synthesis of MNiFeO:
$\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs as template was used to fabricate mesporous MNiFeO thought hybridization and calcination process. First, $_{0.4\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ MOFs was dispersed in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of ethanol, followed by dissolution of 0.2 mmol of HMT with assistant of sonication. Subsequently, $0.1\mathrm{\mmol}$ of $\mathrm{Cu(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ was added to the above mixture with magnetic stirring for $^{9\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Finally, the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}.$ - exchanged $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs was collected, washed, and dried, and it further was calcined at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ with heating rate of $1\ {}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}\ {\mathsf{m i n}}^{-1}$ . The obtain sample named as CuNiFeO.
Besides, equal molar amount of the ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{CH}}_{3}{\mathrm C O O})_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm O},$ , Zn $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0.$ , and $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was adopted to replace $\mathrm{Cu(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ for preparation of CoNiFeO, ZnNiFeO, and MnNiFeO under constant conditions, respectively.
In order to distinguish between temperature effects, the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ - exchanged $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs was further roasted at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $600~^{\circ}C$ , and $700^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , respectively. And these samples were named as CuNiFeO $(500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , CuNiFeO $(600^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , CuNiFeO $(700^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ .
In order to discuss the effect of copper doping amount, 0.025, 0.05, 0.1, and $0.2\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{Cu(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ were used to form ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ -exchanged $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs, respectively. The obtained precursors were further roasted at $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C},$ , and corresponding products were named as CuNiFeO (0.025), CuNiFeO (0.05), CuNiFeO (0.1), and CuNiFeO (0.2).
2.2.3. Synthesis of NiFeO:
$\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs was directly annealed at $500^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ in air for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ with a heating rate of $1\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\ \mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ (Fig. S15a, b).
2.2.4. Synthesis of NiFeO- ${\cdot}V_{o}$ :
$0.8\mathrm{~g~}$ of obtained NiFeO was dispersed in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol containing $_{0.5\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ and treated for $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and resultant powder was washed and dried at $60^{\circ}C$ in vacuum (Fig. S13, 14).
2.2.5. Synthesis of CuO:
HKUST-1 was directly annealed at $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in air for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ with a heating rate of $1\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\ \mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ (Fig. S15c, d).
## 2.3. Characterization
Hitachi U-3010 spectrophotometer using $\mathsf{B a S O}_{4}$ as a reference. Raman spectra were conducted on LabRAM HR evolution with $532~\mathrm{nm}$ wavelength laser source. The transient photocurrent, and EIS performance was examined on a CHI 660E workstation (CHI Instruments Co.) with three electrodes under simulated solar light irradiation (AM1.5 Global filter, $100\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ , Perfect Light solar simulator), 0.5 M ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ aqueous solution as an electrolyte, and all measured potentials were converted to $V$ vs. RHE $\mathit{\check{L}}_{\mathit{R H E}}=\mathit{E}_{\mathrm{Ag/}}$ $_{\mathrm{AgCl}}+0.198+0.0591~\mathrm{pH})$ . The surface area and porosity analyzer was acquired on a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 physisorption instrument by Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) and Barrett-JoynerHalender (BJH) method. Magnetic measurements were researched by vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM) at room temperature on a Micro Sence EV9 (USA). UV–visible absorption spectra (UV–vis) were tested on a Shimadzu UV-2450 spectrophotometer using pure water as a reference.
## 2.4. Catalytic activity measurements
The catalytic performance of MNiFeO NRs was studied by monitoring the changes in maximum absorbance at the wavelength of nitro-compound using a UV–vis spectrometer at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ as the reducing agent. A catalyst suspension was prepared by dispersing $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of MNiFeO NRs in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of water under sonication.
2.4.1. Catalytic reduction of 4-NP:
Firstly, $\boldsymbol{0.1053\mathrm{g}}$ of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ was added into $58~\mathrm{mL}$ of solution of 4-NP with concentration of $0.12~\mathrm{mM}$ , and then $3.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of the mixture was introduced in a standard quartz cell. Subsequently, $_{30\mathrm{uL}}$ of catalyst suspension was rapidly injected into the above mixture, and the max absorption data was recorded. In recycle part, the CuNiFeO were separated with a magnet, washed three times with deionized water and subsequently subjected to the next reaction run.
2.4.2. Catalytic reduction of nitro-compound:
The reduction of other nitro-compounds was similar to 4-NP procedure. Firstly, $0.1053\mathrm{~g~}$ of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ was added into $58~\mathrm{mL}$ of aqueous solution of nitro-compound (4-nitrosophenol: 4-NSP, 2- nitrophenol: 2-NP, 2,4-dinitrophenol: 2,4-DNP, 2,6-dinitrophenol: 2,6-DNP, 2,4,6-trinitrophenol: 2,4,6-TNP, and 4-nitroaniline: 4- NA) with an initial concentration of $0.12~\mathrm{mM}$ , and then $3.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of the mixture was introduced in a standard quartz cell. Subsequently, 30 uL of CuFeNiO suspension was rapidly injected into the above mixture, and the max absorption data was recorded.
## 3. Results and discussion
In view of the fact that the direct pyrolysis of MOFs might produce limited active sites, the rational introduction of guest metal species into MOFs followed by pyrolysis opens up a new path to fabricate porous catalysts with desired activity. Herein, the synthesis of defective MNiFeO NRs with a mesoporous structure was achieved by pyrolysis of cation-exchanged $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs NRs precursors (Scheme 1). First, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs $(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NiO}(00\mathsf{C}{-}\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{H}_{4}{-}$ $\mathbf{\COO})_{3}$ .3DMF) was synthesized by a solvothermal method. Subsequently, a small amount of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions were exchanged in the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2^{-}}$ Ni-MOFs. Lastly, mesoporous CuNiFeO NRs were prepared by pyrolysis of the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ -exchanged $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs in air.
## 3.1. Catalyst characterization
XRD analysis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs confirmed the formation of the MIL-88 phase (Fig. S1a). These well-defined $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MIL-88 NRs with sharp tips had an average aspect ratio of 7 (Fig. 1a, 1b, S1b, and
S1c). In the case of Cu-exchanged $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs, the introduction of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ did not significantly alter the phase and shape of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MIL88 NRs (Fig. S2 and Fig. 1c), except for deepening the powder color (Inset in Fig. 1c). After the thermal treatment of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ -exchanged $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (as determined by TG in Fig. S3), CuNiFeO NRs were generated without obvious shape changes (Fig. 1d). However, the aspect ratio increased to 17:1 with decreasing the rod width due to the thermo-induced shrunk of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs (Fig. 1e). Each nanorod was composed of firmly interconnected nanocrystals with a diameter of $\sim17\:\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1f and 1 g). A HRTEM image in Fig. 1h showed the clear lattice fringes with interplanar distances of 0.251 and $0.208\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which could be indexed to the (311) and (220) planes of NiFeO, respectively. In addition, the proximity lattice fringes with an interplanar distance of $0.252~\mathrm{nm}$ may be assigned to the (311) plane of NiFeO or CuFeO $\mathrm{(CuFe_{2}O_{4})}$ ) due to their similar crystal structure parameter (Fig. S4). As marked by red dashed ovals in Fig. 1i, small and discontinuous pits were observed in CuNiFeO, suggesting the presence of defects. It is routinely supposed that these pit defects can act as active sites due to their accessible coordinationunsaturated atoms with dangling bonds [38]. Abundant grain boundaries were also formed in the nanorods, which effectively increased the density of defect sites. HAADF STEM and line scanning EDX analyses revealed that the order of element density in a nanorod was gradually increasing (Fig. 1j). EDX elemental mapping confirmed the uniform distribution of the Cu, Ni, Fe, and O elements in CuNiFeO (Fig. 1k). The percentage content of Cu in CuNiFeO was determined as $\sim1.5\%$ by ICP. Likewise, other MNiFeO $\mathrm{~\boldmath~{~\cal~M~}~}={\sf C o}$ , Zn, and Mn) NRs were also acquired by the similar procedure to CuNiFeO (Fig. S5).
The crystal phases of NiFeO and MNiFeO $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{\dot{C}}\mathbf{u}$ , Zn, Co, and Mn) NRs were examined by XRD. The XRD pattern of NiFeO NRs is shown in Fig. 2a and can be indexed to the spinel phase of ferrite
NiFeO (JCPDS No. 74–2081). MNiFeO NRs showed a similar XRD profile to NiFeO (Fig. 2a and S6). No characteristic diffraction peaks for $\mathtt{M_{x}O}$ $\mathbf{\check{x}}=1,2\mathbf{\check{\Psi}},$ were observed, likely originating from a low content and high dispersion of the metal species in MNiFeO. Furthermore, simulating structures of NiFeO, CuFeO, and CuNiFeO were similar to each other (Fig. S4), implying less effect of incorporating Cu species on the crystal phase of NiFeO. Raman analysis also supported the structural similarity between NiFeO and CuNiFeO (Fig. 2b). An average crystallite size was calculated to be $16.4~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for CuNiFeO and $15.6\mathrm{{nm}}$ for NiFeO, according to the Scherrer’s formula fixed at $2\Theta=\sim35.6^{\circ}.$ . ${\sf N}_{2}$ desorption measurements revealed that these derived metal oxides with abundant mesoporous, which facilitates rapid diffusion of the reactant molecular (Fig. 2c). And mesoporous NiFeO, NiFeO- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf V}_{\mathrm{o}}$ and CuNiFeO separately possessed a BET surface area of 63.0, 48.4, and $41.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The magnetization saturation (Fig. 2d) of NiFeO, NiFeO- ${\boldsymbol{\cdot}}{\boldsymbol{\nabla}}_{0},$ and CuNiFeO was 18, 28, and 26 emu $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which was smaller than bulk NiFeO (55 emu $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ) [39].
Chemical state analysis of surface elements of NiFeO and CuNiFeO was performed by XPS, as shown in Fig. 3. In the Fe $\mathsf{2p}$ XPS spectra of NiFeO and CuNiFeO (Fig. 3a), the Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ peaks were separately accompanied with a shake-up satellite. And all spin–orbit peaks in Fe 2p regions were best fitted into two characteristic peaks, which were attributed to the $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ cations, respectively. Notably, the binding energy of Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ for CuNiFeO showed a positive shift of $0.55\mathrm{eV}$ compared with that of NiFeO. Such peak shift suggested the decrease in electron density surrounding the Fe species due to the strong electronegativity of the Cu species. The peak broadening of Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ appeared in the XPS spectrum of NiFeO doped with Cu (Fig. 3b). The deconvolution of the Ni 2p spectra revealed the generation of the $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ species in CuNiFeO. The O 1 s spectra of NiFeO and CuNiFeO (Fig. 3c) displayed three characteristic peaks located at
529.9 eV (denoted as O1), $531.6\mathrm{eV}$ (denoted as O2), and $533.4\mathrm{eV}$ (denoted as O3), which can be assigned to lattice oxygen, vacancy sites with low oxygen coordination, and hydroxyl groups or surface-adsorbed oxygen, respectively. Obviously, the content of O2 for NiFeO $(\sim7.94\%)$ increased to $\sim44.24\%$ after the Cu cation doping, suggesting an increase of oxygen vacancies in CuNiFeO.
The above results suggested appreciable interaction between NiFeO and Cu species, and similar phenomena with CuNiFeO were also observed in the Fe 2p, Ni 2p and O1s spectra of CoNiFeO, ZnNiFeO, and MnNiFeO (Fig. S7). EPR as an important tool was also utilized to detect the vacancy at room temperature. As shown in Fig. 3d, the peak intensity of CuNiFeO is significantly higher than that of NiFeO, indicating more unpaired electron/ oxygen vacancies content, which attributed to Cu atoms located next to the chargecompensating oxygen vacancies. The oxidation value of Cu element also was confirmed by combining $\mathtt{C u2p}$ spectra with Cu LM2 spectra. Fig. 3e shows that two major peaks corresponded to the $\mathtt{C u}$ $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathtt{C u2p}_{1/2}$ , accompanying with a satellite peak between 935.5 and 945.5 eV. The binding energies of Cu $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ at $934.92\ \mathrm{eV}$ and Cu $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ at $955.17\ \mathrm{eV}$ with a splitting energy of $19.86\mathrm{eV}$ between the two particular peaks were in agreement with the standard spectrum of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ cation, while $\mathtt{C u2p3}/2$ at 932.47 eV and $\mathtt{C u2p}_{1/2}$ at $952.39\ \mathrm{eV}$ belonged to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ ions or Cu. X-ray Auger spectra of Cu LM2 (Fig. 3f) were deconvoluted into the two peaks of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ located at $918~\mathrm{eV}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ located at $914~\mathrm{eV}.$ .
To reveal the effects of defects and doping on the energy band structure and electron transfer ability of NiFeO, some important optical and electrochemical characterization were performed. Fig. 4a shows UV-DRS spectra of NiFeO, NiFeO- ${\bf\cdot}{\bf V}_{0}$ , and CuNiFeO. The light absorption oneset for NiFeO appeared at about $920~\mathrm{nm}$ and shifted to ${900}\mathrm{nm}$ for NiFeO- ${\bf\cdot}{\bf V}_{\mathrm{o}}$ and $965\mathrm{nm}$ for CuNiFeO. NiFeO is an indirect band gap material, and the optical absorption coefficient near the band edge followed the equation:
$$
a h\nu=A{(h\nu\mathrm{~-~}E_{g})}^{2}
$$
where $a,h,n,E_{g},$ and $A$ are the absorption coefficient, plank’s constant, light frequency, band gap, and proportionality constant, respectively [40]. $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ was estimated to be $1.43\ \mathrm{eV}$ for NiFeO- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf V}_{0}$ and $1.5\mathrm{eV}$ for CuNiFeO (Fig. 4b). Valence band edge $(E_{\mathsf{V B}})$ was identified by extrapolation of the linear region of each VB-XPS plots in Fig. 4c. The $\scriptstyle{E_{\mathrm{VB}}}$ values of NiFeO, NiFeO- ${\bf\cdot}{\bf V}_{0}$ , and CuNiFeO was 0.96, 0.87, and $1.05\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. Using the equation, $E_{\mathrm{CB}}=E_{\mathrm{VB}}-E_{\mathrm{g}},$ gave the conduction band edges of 0.57 eV (NiFeO), $-0.56\ \mathrm{eV}$ (NiFeO- ${\bf\cdot}{\bf V}_{0,\ast}$ ), and 0.45 eV (CuNiFeO). Accordingly, the band diagrams of NiFeO, NiFeO- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf V}_{\mathrm{o}}$ , and CuNiFeO are drawn in Fig. 4d. CuNiFeO was observed to have a lower energy band than NiFeO and NiFeO- ${\cdot}V_{\circ}$ . Transient photocurrent responses (Fig. 4e) showed that the charge separation of CuNiFeO was slightly enhanced in comparison with NiFeO. EIS analysis manifested the enhanced charge transport of NiFeO upon the $\mathtt{C u}$ ions introduced (Fig. 4f).
Here, $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ -TPR was employed to investigate the redox property of CuO, CuNiFeO, and NiFeO (Fig. 5a). $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and partial $\mathsf{F e}^{\bar{3}+}$ in NiFeO were separately reduced to metallic Ni $(311~^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ and multivalent $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ $(497^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ , and the later was further transformed to metallic Fe $(587~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ though the route of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ -FeO-Fe [41,42]. The reduction of the $\mathtt{C u O}$ occurred in two consecutive steps: $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}(\mathsf{C u O})\to\mathsf{C u}^{+}$ $(\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ and $\mathsf{C u O}\to\mathsf{C u}$ (metal), and blue curve showed one peak energy at $408~^{\circ}C$ for the reduction of $\mathtt{C u O}$ It was seen from red curve that three distinctive reduction peaks and a broad peak were at different regions. The first peak at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ was assigned to surface adsorbed monatomic species $(0^{-})$ and peroxy species $(0_{2}^{-})$ [43], which is consistent with the observation from the O1s XPS and ESR spectra. The next peak at $178^{\circ}\mathsf C$ corresponded to the reduction of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ to $\mathtt{C u}$ [44]. The other reduction peaks in the ranges of $210{-}450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $450\mathrm{-}750~^{\circ}C,$ , could be ascribed to the reduction of NiFeO to Ni and transformation of NiFeO to Fe, respectively. Compared with $\mathtt{C u O}$ and NiFeO, the reduction temperatures of CuNiFeO systematically shifted to lower regions, suggesting CuNiFeO with lower reducibility. Electron transfer of NiFeO, CuO and CuNiFeO was further examined by EPR coupled with the spin probe CPH (Fig. 5b). Among the tested three catalysts, CuNiFeO exhibited the highest intensity of a triplet signal, indicting its enhancement of electron transfer. This enhancement may be attributed to synergistic effects among $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{C u}^{+}$ , $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}/\mathsf{N i}^{2+},$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ redox pairs.
## 3.2. Catalytic performance evaluation
## 3.2.1. Catalytic reduction of 4-NP
The catalytic performance of different samples was first evaluated using a typical reduction of aqueous 4-NP in the presence of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ . As shown in Fig. S16, the absorption peak of 4-NP at $313~\mathrm{nm}$ shifted to $400~\mathrm{{nm}}$ after adding $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ , resulting from the formation of 4-nitrophenolate in a basic solution, and the light yellow solution of 4-NP also turned deeper. 4-NP was hardly reduced by $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ within $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , as indicated by the constant absorption peak at $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S16a). In contrast, CuNiFeO enabled the 4- NP reduction process to proceed quickly (Fig. S16b). In order to observe the catalytic process more intuitively, the dynamic curve method was used to record their intensity of absorption peak of 4-nitrophenolate.
The catalytic performance of $_{\mathsf{N i F e O-V_{o}}}$ and MNiFeO $\mathbf{\nabla}[\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , Zn, Co, Cu) was compared with pristine NiFeO as summarized in Fig. $6(\mathsf{a},\mathsf{b})$ . Given $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ was large excess of 4-NP $\tan_{(\mathrm{NaBH4})}$ : $n_{(4\cdot}$ $_{\mathrm{NP)}}=400{:}1_{.}^{\cdot}$ ), the reduction of 4-NP matched pseudo first-order kinetics well and the kinetics equation can be expressed as follows:
$$
\ln{(C/C_{0})}\ =k_{a p p}t
$$
where $k_{a p p}$ is apparent rate constant, t is the reaction time, and C and $C_{0}$ are defined as the concentrations of 4-NP at time t and at $t=0$ , respectively. Fig. 6b shows the linear relationship between $\ln(C/C_{0})$ vs t of NiFeO based catalyst. The apparent $k_{a p p}$ was calculated from the slopes of linearly fitted plots and corresponding value was an important parameter to compare the performance of catalysts. A catalyst activity trend in terms of kinetic rate constant was follows: $\mathrm{CuNiFeO}>\mathrm{CoNiFeO}>\mathrm{ZnNiFeO}>\mathrm{~MnNiFeO}>\mathrm{Ni}$ $\mathrm{FeO-V_{o}\Sigma>}$ NiFeO. The catalytic activity of MNiFeO catalysts was higher than that of NiFeO- ${\cdot}V_{\circ}$ and NiFeO, indicating that catalytic activity of NiFeO can be improved though optimizing catalytic component and creating oxygen defect.
Pristine NiFeO manifested rather poor catalytic activity due to poor capability of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ redox couple for electron transfer. Its catalytic performance can only be slightly enhanced through the surface reduction with $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ . This enhancement may be attributed to the contribution of surface oxygen vacancy to the adsorption of 4-nitrophenolate and borohydride anions [30,34]. The introduction of metal species allowed for the enhanced performance of the NiFeO catalyst. Such enhancement benefited from new redox couples (such as $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{C u}$ $\mathsf{C u}^{+}/\mathsf{C u}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}/{\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/{\mathsf{C o}}$ , $Z\mathrm n^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}$ , $\mathsf{M n}^{3+}/\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ , or $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}/\mathsf{M n})$ and the creation of oxygen vacancies. According to Fig. 6b, CuNiFeO had the highest activity among all the MNiFeO catalysts. It may be empirically found that the easier the metal ions are reduced to metallic form, the better the catalytic performance of their oxides. Compared with $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ $(\mathrm{E^{0}(M n/M n^{2+})}^{-}=\mathrm{~-~}1.18~\mathrm{eV}),\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}\left(\mathrm{E^{0}(Z n/Z n^{2+})}=\mathrm{~-~}0.76~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ , and $\mathrm{CO}^{2+}\ \mathrm{(E^{0}(C O/C O^{2+})=\mathrm{~-~}0.28~e V)}$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions $(\mathrm{E^{0}}(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+})=0.34~\mathrm{eV})$ are more likely to be reduced into metallic species. Meanwhile, these reduced Cu species can also be oxidized quickly by the active oxygen in the solution, and released electrons further transfer to 4- nitrophenolate ions adsorbed on the surface. Both reduction and oxidation process are repeated along with the whole hydrogenation process, named ‘‘redox cycle”, which is a common phenomenon in aqueous reduction reaction [45]. As a result, the Cu species exhibited the strongest electron relaying capacity.
Although the conversion for ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}/{\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ $(\mathrm{E}^{0}(\mathsf{C o}^{3+}/\mathsf{C o}^{2+})=+1.81\ \mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathrm{Mn^{3+}/M n^{2+}}~(\mathrm{E^{0}/M n^{3+}/M n^{2+}})=+1.51~\mathrm{eV})$ is relatively easy, the lower number of electrons transferred by them per unit time leads to limited contribution in the catalytic process.
The catalytic performance of the CuNiFeO NRs prepared at different temperatures (500, 600, and $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ was also examined, as shown in Fig. 6(c, d). The one prepared at $500^{\circ}\mathsf C(0.037s^{-1};$ exhibited a higher catalytic activity toward the reduction of 4-NP than those at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}(0.019s^{-1}\cdot$ and at $700^{\circ}\mathsf{C}(0.011s^{-1})$ . The good performance of the CuNiFeO (5 0 0) catalyst was attributed to its small particle size and abundant defects in the surface (Fig. S9-11, Table S1,2). The effect of copper content in CuNiFeO for catalytic performance was also revealed in Fig. 6(e, f). With the increase of Cu content, the value of rate constant first increased and then decreased. When the Cu content was $0.1\ \mathrm{mmol}$ , the rate constant of catalyst reaches the maximum. Exceeding the optimum amount of doping may lead to phase separation and thus reduce their activity (Fig. S12). To illustrate this phenomenon, the catalytic performance of the $\mathtt{C u O}$ and CuNiFeO was contrasted in Fig. $6(\mathbf{g},\mathbf{h})$ . The conversion of 4-NP achieved up $90\%$ by $\mathtt{C u O}$ in $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Although the content of copper in CuNiFeO was about $1.5\%$ , its activity exceeded that of CuO, indicative of the cooperative interaction of Cu species and NiFeO. Compared with $\mathtt{C u O}$ alone, the Cu species dispersed in NiFeO NRs had much smaller size and was more conducive to expose active sites. Meanwhile, CuNiFeO also showed lower reducibility and comparable conductivity than that of CuO, that is, it was more suitable for reduction of 4-NP to 4-AP [46,47]. In addition, the Cu cation-induced oxygen vacancy facilitated the adsorption of 4-nitrophenolate and borohydride anions.
In heterogeneous catalysis, the turnover frequency (TOF) was regard as an important indicator to assess catalytic efficiency, and it was calculated by dividing the amount of 4-NP reduced per mole of NiFeO per unit time. The TOF and constant values of NiFeO based catalysts was summarized in Table 1. The TOF values of CuNiFeO was three hundred- to four hundred-fold superior to those of NiFeO- ${\bf\cdot}{\bf V}_{\mathrm{o}}$ , and NiFeO, respectively. Moreover, CuNiFeO exhibited the higher TOF of $2.89\times10^{5}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and specific constant $K$ of $7.32\times10^{5}\mathrm{\min}^{-1}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ than many noble metals in previous reports, such as Au, Ag, Pt and Pd. The catalytic reduction of 4- NP over CuNiFeO was investigated at 298.15 K, 303.15 K, $^{308.15\mathrm{~K},}$ and $313.15\mathrm{~K~}$ to determine $k$ at these temperatures (Fig.S17). Based on the linear fit of the Arrhenius plot (lnk vs $1/\l$ T), the apparent activation energy $\left(E_{a}\right)$ was calculated to be $68.39\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ .
## 3.2.2. Stability of the catalysts
The reusability of CuNiFeO catalyst has been also evaluated for reduction of 4-NP to 4-AP with assistance of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ in eight cycles. The CuNiFeO was rapidly separated by a magnet after the end of the first reaction, the same amount of 4-NP and $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ were replaced in reaction system for the succession runs. Fig. 7a shows that the time required to convert the same amount of 4-NP gradually increases as the reaction continues to run, and this difference in performance may be due to the loss of catalyst during the recovery process. The CuNiFeO used for eight times was recovered, washed and dried for further study its stability by characterization technologies. In contrast to fresh CuNiFeO, there was few variations in large rate of length to width after reaction in Fig. 7b. The crystal structure and surface condition of the used CuNiFeO also were surveyed by XRD and XPS measurements. The slight reduce in the intensity of peaks was presented after reaction compared with those fresh CuNiFeO (Fig. 7c). The XPS spectra of $\mathtt{C u2p}$ Ni 2p, and Fe 2p core levels were obtained from Fig. 7(d, e, f). The elements of Cu, Ni and Fe in both fresh and used CuNiFeO catalysts existed in the form of $\mathsf{C u}^{+}/\mathsf{C u},\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ ; $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ ; $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ . For fresh and spent CuNiFeO, and a slight peak shift and spectra variation of the Cu, Ni and Fe element were noticed, originating that metal ions were in a dynamic equilibrium during the reaction. The above analysis showed that CuNiFeO had good stability in the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ -riched solution, which also indicated CuNiFeO NRs potential application in the catalytic conversion of nitro compounds to the corresponding amines.
## 3.2.3. Mechanistic insight into the reduction
It is generally believed that 4-nitrophenolate ions undergo the successive transformation of the two intermediates 4- nitrosophenol and 4-hydroxyaminophenol, and then convert into 4-AP during hydrogenation [54]. The whole process involves both electron transfer and proton transport. Electrons can be usually donated from the dissociation of $B\mathrm{H}_{4}^{-}$ ions. However, it is controversial whether the proton supplier is $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ or $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ [55]. Based on our previous discussion, we continue to assume $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the proton supplier for understanding the reduction of 4-NP over the CuNiFeO catalyst (Scheme 2) [56]: First, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ dissociated from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is reduced with an electron from $B\mathrm{H}_{4}^{-}$ , forming an active hydrogen species $\left(\mathsf{H}_{\mathsf{a b s.}}\right)$ adsorbed on the catalyst surface. Next, Two $\mathrm{H_{abs}}$ atoms are then attacked by the nitro group $(-\mathsf{N O}_{2})$ in the neighbouring 4- nitrophenolate ion. The first intermediate 4-nitrosophenolate is produced by removing a $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule. Repeating the hydrogenaddition and oxygen-removal process, the product 4-AP is generated from the second intermediate (4-hydroxyaminophenol).
In the current study, the introduction of $\mathtt{C u}$ species leads to highly active redox couples $(\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{C u}$ and $\mathsf{C u^{+}/C u)}$ , energy band changes of NiFeO, and increases oxygen vacancies. Accordingly, the Cu exchanged significantly improves the electron transfer from $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ to 4-nitrophenolate ions over NiFeO. Apparent evidence from EIS showed that CuNiFeO had the charge transportation resistance comparable to pure $\mathtt{C u O}$ and higher than NiFeO. ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions exchanged in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOFs are confined to grow up and aggregate during the heat transformation [57]. This confinement promotes the high dispersion of the Cu species in NiFeO NRs, facilitating to expose active sites for adsorption and conversion of substrate molecules. The down shift of energy band for CuNiFeO relative to NiFeO may increase the surface affinity to electrons. This induced oxygen vacancies may additionally contribute to increasing charge transportation and capture anions in the surface of catalysts [30].
## 3.2.4. Catalytic reduction of nitroarenes
To further survey the general applicability of CuNiFeO catalyst in the reduction of nitroaromatics to aromatic amines, a variety of nitroarenes with diverse structures were choose as the probe molecules. As shown in Fig. S18, it was obviously observed from a series of the successive UV–vis spectra that CuNiFeO facilitated the reduction of nitroaromatics to corresponding aniline products in the presence of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ , and the absorbance peak intensity of the nitroarenes (4-NSP at $553\ \mathrm{nm}$ , 2-NP at $425\ \mathrm{nm}$ , 2,4-DNP at $375\ \mathrm{nm}$ , 2,6-DNP at $428\ \mathrm{nm}$ , 2,4,6-TNP at $384~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , and 4-NA at $378\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) gradually decreased with reaction time. The reduction of nitroaromatics was recorded by using the maximum absorbance of wavelength as signal. It was acquired from plots of ${C}/{C_{0}}$ vs t in Fig. 8(a-f) that the conversion of nitroaromatics to corresponding aniline products was achieved by CuNiFeO in 40 to $150s.$ implying superior catalytic performance. As reference, CuO NPs showed lower catalytic activity than CuNiFeO. It was also concluded from Fig. 8(g, h) that CuNiFeO exhibited excellent catalytic efficiency than CuO, which further illustrated that the former was more suitable for the reduction of nitro-compounds. The difference of reaction rate for the reduction of those nitro-compounds by $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ using same catalyst can be related to the conjugation effect, inductive effect and steric effect of the substituent group [58,59].
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, defective MNiFeO $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ , Zn, Co, Mn) NRs were synthesized via cation-exchange and calcination strategy using the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ -MOF template, which presented obviously increased catalytic activity for the reduction of nitroaromatic. The introduction of exotic metal ion not only supplys redox couples, but also regulates electric distribution and induces massive defects in NiFeO, which further optimizes band gap, decreases reducibility and enhances conductivity. In contrast to NiFeO or NiFeO- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf V}_{0}$ , MNiFeO can offer more redox pairs participated in reaction, and thus they always show excellent performance in the reduction of 4- NP. Especially for CuNiFeO, the reason for outstanding activity maybe closely related to the higher redox potential of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{C u}^{+}$ pairs, and it also presents good reusability and stability in the succession runs. Moreover, CuNiFO as a catalyst achieves high conversion rate in the reduction of a series of nitroarenes. Therefore, mesoporous CuNiFeO NRs exhibit the potential to substitute noble metals catalyst for reduction of nitro compounds to corresponding anilines in the fields of industrial production and environmental remediation. This work highlights a comprehensive understanding on the origin of high catalytic activity of spinel and provides a convenient strategy to develop advanced catalysts based on synergistically cation and defect engineering.
